:href "/venues/2173"} "Grand Palais Éphémère, Paris, France"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/2007346"} "26 from 26 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Japan’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/144396"} "Akira Sone"] " was the gold medalist at Tokyo 2020 and was looking to become the first woman to defend a gold medal in this class. She was World Champion in 2019 and 2023. France’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/142749"} "Romane Dicko"] " won bronze in the class at Tokyo, was the World Champion in 2022, and had won European titles in 2018, 2020, 2022, and 2023. Another contender was Brazilian " [:a {:href "/athletes/153205"} "Beatriz Souza"] ", who had five gold medals in this event at the Pan American Championships, including the last four consecutively (2021-24)."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Sone was upset in the quarter-finals by Turkey’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/131907"} "Kayra Özdemir"] ", who used " [:em {} "kami-shiho-gatame"] " to win by " [:em {} "waza-ari"] " in extra time at 5:48. Souza defeated Dicko in the semi-finals by " [:em {} "ippon"] ", using " [:em {} "kesa-gatame"] " in extra time, and advanced to the final against Israeli " [:a {:href "/athletes/143941"} "Raz Hershko"] ", bronze medalist at the 2023 World Championships, who quickly beat Özdemir by " [:em {} "ippon"] " at 0:14 in the other semi-final. Souza won the gold, defeating Hershko by " [:em {} "waza-ari"] " at 0:44, using " [:em {} "o-soto-guruma"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The bronze medals went to Dicko and Korean " [:a {:href "/athletes/153310"} "Kim Ha-Yun"] ", both winning their bronze medal matches by " [:em {} "ippon"] ". Dicko repeated her bronze medal performance to win the fifth medal in this class for France, while Kim’s medal was only the second in the class for Korea, after a bronze in 2000 by " [:a {:href "/athletes/92361"} "Kim Seon-Yeong"] "."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [: